  "content": {
    "id": "p16-q57-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "Show that $\\dfrac1{xy-y^2}+\\dfrac1{xy-x^2}=\\dfrac1{xy}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac1{xy}$, for real $x,y$ with $x\\ne0$, $y\\ne0$ and $x\\ne y$.\n\nFactorise the denominators, use $y-x=-(x-y)$ and combine.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac1{xy-y^2}+\\frac1{xy-x^2}&=\\frac1{y(x-y)}+\\frac1{x(y-x)}\\\\\\frac1{xy-y^2}+\\frac1{xy-x^2}&=\\frac{x}{xy(x-y)}-\\frac{y}{xy(x-y)}\\\\\\frac1{xy-y^2}+\\frac1{xy-x^2}&=\\frac{x-y}{xy(x-y)}\\\\\\frac1{xy-y^2}+\\frac1{xy-x^2}&=\\frac1{xy}\\end{align*}$$\nOriginal domain: real $x,y$ with $x\\ne0$, $y\\ne0$ and $x\\ne y$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 42
  },
